    "available_at": "2026-11-10T20:50:31.492553+00:00",
    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Has a Taste of the e-MTB World with the RN0 Motor-Assisted Bicycle\nNew Hot Wheels Elite 64 Hauler Is a 1979 Chevy C30 Crew Cab Dually 4×4 That Will Cost $65\n19 photos\nPhoto: Hot Wheels Malaysia\nI went shopping today and stopped by a few toy stores again. One of them had just brought in the Car Culture: Road Trip mix and I was excited for a moment until I saw the price. They had ramped it up from $9 per piece to $13. Good luck with that! I walked away knowing I could simply get them online for as little as $8 per car.\nThat might not sound like a big difference, but it can easily add up when you’re buying hundreds of these per year. Also, in hindsight, Road Trip wasn’t all that exciting. I found a new peg-warmer, too, from the Aerostyles mix. And no, it wasn’t the Custom ‘70 Chevy Nova, but the LB-Kaido Works Nissan R32 Skyline GT-R.\nMeanwhile, I’m still shaking with excitement knowing that I somehow got my hands on the black 2008 Audi A4 Touring Car: I’ll unbox it for a closer look soon! Until then, I’ve found some new photos of the Premium F1 collection for 2027. We’re moving up to replicas of the 2026 cars and you’ll notice that the card art now features a photo of the driver, too.\nThat should help with sales, at least for some of the top drivers. I guess this might be your last chance to get your replica of Esteban Ocon ’s F1 machine, as he might not get another chance in the sport. That’s a story for another time, though, as right now I’d like to take you over to Mattel’s Elite 64 series. One of the most highly-anticipated collectibles for the line is just around the corner.\nWe’ve seen teaser photos and videos for the 1979 Chevy C30 Crew Cab Dually 4x4 for some time, but now is your chance to go after it without having to spend a small fortune. Well, the price is obviously higher than with any other Elite 64 (and even RLC ) release, because the package itself is rather huge for a 1/64th-scale release.\nAt $65, you get Phil Riehlman’s brand-new Chevrolet C/K truck plus a two-level trailer that can transport up to five cars. The previous two haulers were technically ready to transport six models, although I’ve never had a chance to test that myself. The Freightliner Cascadia originally sold for $50, while the Scania 770 S was slightly more expensive a $55.\nSome sellers on eBay will try to charge you as much as $450 for both. I’d suggest trying to get them for less than $150, or aim for other nicer options that aren’t over-hyped. I’m itching for a closer look at some of Ixo’s offerings.\nMini GT and Tarmac Works also have some solutions for that itch, and I guess the best way to go about it would be for me to order a few of them from each brand for a head-to-head comparison.\nAnyway, the Duramax Diesel engine under the hood of the C30 is one of the main attraction points, alongside the tire/wheel setup on the rear axle. While I encourage Mattel to bring more pickup trucks to Team Transport, I’d rather Elite 64 Haulers follow the same path as they did for the first two years.\nWe know for sure that’s how things will be going down in 2027, which is great. The 1979 Chevy C30 Crew Cab Dually 4x4 goes live on Mattel Creations on November 12 2026 at 9 AM PT. Am I the only one who thinks it will be sold out within the first 60 seconds?\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHot Wheels Elite 64 Chevrolet pickup truck\nAbout the author: Dragos Chitulescu\nThe things Dragos enjoys the most in life are, in no particular order: cars, motorcycles, diecast cars, and drifting. He's seen (and driven) many vehicles since he started his writing career back in 2009, but his garage currently houses a 1991 Mazda RX-7 FC3S Turbo II and a 1999 Suzuki SV650-S. Full profile\nUP NEXT The Ford Explorer's Latest NHTSA Investigation Covers 1.2 Million Vehicles",
